<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Jerba</h1><p class="st-meta">1284 CE – 1335 CE · capital Jerba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Catalan and Sicilian lordship over the Tunisian island of Jerba, held by Christian admirals for half a century until a local revolt.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1284, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">War of the Sicilian Vespers</a>, the admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-of-lauria">Roger of Lauria</a>, serving the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Jerba</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate</a>, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerkennah-islands">Kerkennah</a> islands. The pope recognised Lauria's lordship, and he fortified the island with the castle known as the Castello.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A small Christian garrison held the castle while the Ibadi Muslim islanders paid tribute and kept their own law and religion. Revolts were frequent. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-of-lauria">Lauria</a> heirs' rule faltered, the island passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a>, and the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-muntaner">Ramon Muntaner</a> governed it from 1311 to about 1315, describing his rule in his Chronicle.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A revolt of the islanders, backed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsids</a>, overwhelmed the garrison in 1335 and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid</a> rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Jerba</a> remained contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> into the 16th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1284 CE – 1335 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.25%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1284 CE – 1335 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jerba</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Roger of Lauria, Ramon Muntaner</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ibadi Islam (islanders); Catholic Christianity (rulers)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber, Arabic, Catalan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Jerba, Jerba and Kerkennah</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1310&amp;focus=lordship-of-jerba" aria-label="Lordship of Jerba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4916 963 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Jerba lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5333 1064h0M5255 1107h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5236 1219h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5236 1219h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Tunisia. <a href="/atlas?year=1310&amp;focus=lordship-of-jerba">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

